<figure class="field-study-figure supply-figure">
  <h2>The economics of paddy straw</h2>
  <div class="supply-chain">
    <div class="chain-step">
      <span class="step-number">01</span>
      <strong>Smallholder<br />farmers</strong>
      <p>Fragmented landholdings; a short, fixed clearing window.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div class="chain-step">
      <span class="step-number">02</span>
      <strong>Local<br />aggregators</strong>
      <p>Shared machinery access and coordination costs.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div class="chain-step">
      <span class="step-number">03</span>
      <strong>Baling and<br />transportation</strong>
      <p>Bulky material; costs rise quickly beyond 50 km.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div class="chain-step">
      <span class="step-number">04</span>
      <strong>Storage<br />facilities</strong>
      <p>Seasonal supply requires land and fire-safe storage.</p>
    </div>
    <span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
    <div class="chain-step">
      <span class="step-number">05</span>
      <strong>Compressed<br />biogas plants</strong>
      <p>Capital-intensive processing; reliable feedstock required.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="constraint-band">
    <span>Key constraints</span>
    <p>Fragmented supply · Machinery costs · Transportation costs · Seasonal availability · Storage requirements · Capital-intensive processing</p>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    <b>Figure 3.</b> A simplified paddy-straw supply chain. CEEW estimates put a 50 km delivered baled-straw cost at roughly ₹2,500–₹3,000 per tonne and describe distances beyond 50 km as commercially challenging.
    Source: Council on Energy, Environment and Water, <i>Is Ex-situ Crop Residue Management a Scalable Solution to Stubble Burning?</i>, 2021.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

